A -- modify(13) aCTionDefinE(4) setLimits setMacroValue reportMaxLimit
     materialiseTerms. These run at DEFINE time on fields being defined from
     incant source. A specimen is COPIED, never defined, so it cannot arrive
     here. Mark 3's producer-side assert owns this family's invariant.
B -- parseAction(5) parseSetLabel(3) parseScaf*(10) parseJSON*(6) parseRule
     parseAny parseSet parseString parseUpTo parseContainer parseCondition
     parseCharacter. SELF-EXCLUDING, and this is the load-bearing sentence:
     they are dispatched THROUGH rStuff->parseMethod, so a node with no
     rStuff cannot reach them -- the dispatch is the guard.
C -- test*(8) getWhatFollows(2) getGuard checkInput and friends. These are
     handed a RuleStuff the walk already holds.
G -- aCTionCodE aCTionStatemenT. Subjects are registered grammar rules.

THE THREE CANDIDATE ROWS, for Tony to mark

1. processFlags  GroupRules.mm:11665   -- REACHABLE (YES)
       if ( target->groupBody->flags.isRule )
               target->rStuff->notifyFail = 1;
   ⚠ GUARDED BY THE WRONG FLAG, and it is case 36's shape exactly. isRule
   lives in groupBody, which copyOf copies wholesale, so a twin is isRule
   and has no rStuff -- the guard passes and the deref dies. processFlags
   is bound as an incant command, so a fixture CAN hand it a specimen. No
   current caller does. The defect is that the guard tests the wrong thing,
   not that someone is calling it wrong.

2. processCode   GroupRules.mm:11572   -- CAN'T-TELL
       if ( field->groupBody->flags.isLabel )
               field = field->rStuff->rule;
   Same wrong-flag shape (isLabel is a groupBody flag and is copied), and
   this one sits ON THE SPECIMEN PATH -- compile() calls processCode, and
   station 5 goes through it. It does not fire today only because a Braced
   twin is not isLabel. It is ONE FLAG away. Whether the crucible ever mints
   an isLabel specimen is a question about the campaign's direction, which
   is Tony's to answer, not mine to default.

3. aCTionFailed  GroupRules.mm:834     -- CAN'T-TELL
       ::printf(... ::getDebugText(input->rStuff->failedAt,40));
   Fires when a parse FAILS. If a specimen is ever the subject of a failing
   parse, the failure report itself dies -- which would replace a
   diagnostic with a crash, the worst possible place for one. Whether
   specimens get parsed as subjects is the same open question as row 2.

